'''Farisnimo''' or '''Farism'''  was an early working-class reform movement in [[Burawa]] during the [[Early Republic Era]]. It emerged in the late 1830s among dockworkers, miners, railway labourers, and shipyard employees, who argued that the reforms associated with the [[Rose Revolution]] and the [[Constitutional Convention of 1832]] had not adequately protected wage earners or restrained the influence of the merchant and industrial classes. Farists advocated labour protections, political liberalism, and broader suffrage, and the movement became one of the earliest organised expressions of industrial politics in republican Burawa.

== Etymology ==

The Burawi term ''Farisnimo'' derived from the Burawi word ''faris'', meaning "seat" or "place of representation". Early organisers used the word to argue that labourers should have seats in the institutions of the republic comparable to those held by merchants and landowners. English-language writers later rendered the movement's name as Farism. By the 1840s, the term had come to designate the wider reform movement.

Farisnimo developed during the first phase of Burawa's industrial expansion, when new investment in shipbuilding, mining, rail transport, and port infrastructure created an expanding urban working class. Many early Farists were artisans or skilled labourers who derived limited benefit from the constitutional settlement of 1832. They criticised the property-based franchise, the concentration of political influence in the hands of the merchant and industrial classes, and the absence of statutory protections for hours, wages, and workplace safety. Farist meetings were often held in reading rooms, union lodges, and informal workers' associations in [[Samara]], [[Mazar]], [[Jowhar]], and other industrial centres.

== Organisation and ideology ==

Farisnimo was not a single political party or a fully centralised organisation. It was instead a broad reformist current that linked local workers' associations, mutual aid societies, petitioning committees, and early trade unions. Its principal institutional expression was the [[Burawa Workers Union]], which emerged in the 1840s as a federation of sectoral unions in mining, rail transport, dock labour, and shipbuilding. The union never functioned as a nationwide body in the modern sense, but it gave the movement a recognisable organisational core and enabled coordination across several industrial regions.

Farist writers and organisers argued that political rights should not be restricted to property holders. Their demands for suffrage were initially framed in universalist language, though most early Farist leaders still assumed male political participation as the norm. The movement also called for regular payment of wages, limits on arbitrary dismissal, compensation for workplace injury, and reduced working hours. In practice, these demands addressed the daily conditions of labour in Burawa's expanding industrial towns rather than abstract constitutional theory.

Early Farist tracts circulated widely in handwritten and cheaply printed form. These included [[Aadan Warsame]]'s ''The Republic Must Feed Its Hands'', [[Suleymaan Dheer]]'s ''On the Wage Question'', and [[Fadumo Warsame]]'s ''The Franchised Workshop''. These writings argued that republican liberty had limited meaning for labourers who remained dependent on unstable pay, unsafe worksites, and political exclusion. Later historians often described the texts as proto-socialist, but their authors were more commonly identified in their own time as radical reformers or labour republicans.

== Industrial protest and the Jowhar Riots ==

Farisnimo gained visibility through a wave of industrial unrest in the 1840s and 1850s. Strikes and demonstrations were reported in the mining districts of the interior, among railway crews working on the early trunk lines, and in the shipyards and waterfronts of the major ports. In several cities, workers staged protests over weekly wage payments, accident compensation, and the use of hired guards to break collective action. These disputes were not confined to a single region; they formed part of a broader pattern of republican-era labour agitation.

The most prominent violent episode associated with the movement was the [[Jowhar Riots]]. The riots began after a dockworker was killed by a hired security officer during a dispute over weekly payment at the [[Jowhar Shipbuilding Corporation]]. The killing triggered a mass demonstration that escalated into several days of violence between workers, company guards, and local authorities. The unrest spread from the dock district to nearby workshops and storage yards, and it became associated with the wider conflict between labour and capital in the early republic.

The riots increased Farisnimo's visibility among industrial workers and encouraged a more organised response from employers, who increasingly negotiated with union delegates rather than rely solely on private security. The Burawi state treated the riots as both a public order crisis and a warning that the republic's industrial economy was producing new forms of social tension. Subsequent labour petitions frequently cited Jowhar as evidence that wage disputes could no longer be dismissed as local disturbances.

== The Free Women's Movement ==

By the 1850s, a feminist current had developed within Farisnimo. Women employed in textiles, clerical work, teaching, and food processing argued that the movement's original suffrage demands remained incomplete if political rights were limited to men. Activists such as [[Fadumo Warsame]] and [[Maryan Cabdi]] became associated with this current, which pressed for genuine universal suffrage as well as protections for women workers. Their organisers collaborated with male Farists, but they also insisted that labour reform and political equality could not be separated from the question of women's civic status.

In later historiography, this current is identified as the beginning of the [[Free Women's Movement in Burawa]]. Its supporters argued that the republic had produced a new commercial elite without sufficiently extending political citizenship, and that women wage earners were doubly excluded: first as workers and then as women. The movement retained the gender assumptions of its period, but it represented the first sustained challenge within Burawa to the male exclusivity of political reform.

== Legacy ==

Farisnimo declined as a distinct movement by the late 1850s, but its ideas persisted through trade unions, municipal reform circles, and the growing literature of labour politics. The Burawa Workers Union survived in altered form as sectoral organisation became more formalised, and many of the movement's demands were later absorbed into wider campaigns for industrial regulation and democratic expansion. Its insistence that republican rights should include labour protections and broader suffrage made it an important precursor to later reform currents in Burawi politics.

Later Burawi historians have described Farisnimo as the first organised expression of class politics in the republic. Its pamphleteers, organisers, and strike leaders helped define a language of labour rights that reappeared in later socialist writing, while the women's current that developed within it provided an early foundation for Burawa's suffrage debates. Although Farisnimo never became a mass party, it established a durable connection between industrial labour, political liberalism, and reformist agitation in the early republic.

